 n-conversation-with-prof-josh-landy\n\nThe French and Italian Department a
 nd French Speaking Worlds: Here and Now present: A Book talk in English on
  First They Came for Words\, les Arènes\, 2026\, by Prof. Cécile Alduy\,
  in conversation with Prof. Joshua Landy. \n\nA New Linguistic and Politic
 al Regime\n\n“Women\,” “climate crisis\,” “disability\,” “di
 versity\,” “Black\,” “equality\,” “gender\,” “social justi
 ce\,” “LGBT\,” “prejudice\,” “racism\,” “vaccine” ... Th
 ese words\, among many others\, have been banned or blacklisted by the Tru
 mp administration since March 2025. In her book\, First They Came for Word
 s\, Cécile Alduy conducts a gripping investigation into this list\, which
  grew from a handful of demonized terms into over 480 flammable ones throu
 gh anticipatory obedience\, self-censorship\, and overcompliance. From res
 earch labs to classrooms\, libraries\, archives\, medical information\, di
 saster prep documents\, monuments\, corporate websites and even natural pa
 rks\, hundreds of words vanished\, or were used as weapons against science
 \, advocates\, communities or political adversaries.  \n\nA professor of l
 iterature at Stanford and a discourse analysis scholar at Sciences Po\, C
 écile Alduy has spent years analyzing how the far-right wages its culture
  war. On January 20\, 2025\, her research caught up with her brutally. At 
 Stanford\, her life and work were suddenly upended\, like those of colleag
 ues\, students and workers in universities\, schools\, agencies\, and scie
 ntific labs nationwide\, when the new administration unleashed brutal cens
 orship on cu
